  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Thursday)\nIssued at 742 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nNorthwest flow will become dominant aloft as the aforementioned\ntrough responsible for the rain over the weekend begins to exit the\neastern CONUS and upper ridging builds in ahead of our next system.\nAfter overcast skies and rain on Sunday, temperatures will rebound a\nbit on Monday with the building ridge to our west. Sfc high pressure\nover the Appalachians will keep dry NE flow in place and may be\nenough to result in clearing skies by Monday afternoon. A closed\nupper low will dig across Oklahoma and into the lower MS River Valley\nTuesday into Wednesday, bringing increasing chances for rain and a\nfew thunderstorms. Model trends over the past several days have been\nfairly inconsistent with this storm system, with today's runs\ncontinuing to slow the system down as well as pulling the sfc low\nfarther south. Since the timing and evolution of this system will\nhave big impacts on rainfall amounts and any potential for strong\nstorms, we do not feel particularly confident in any specific details\nat this time. What we can speak to is the continued trend with a\nslower system, increasing our confidence that impacts to Election Day\nshould be minimal with only low chances for rain (15-25%) at this \ntime. Temperatures will remain near or just above normal for much of\nthe long term, with highs in the 70s and lows in the 50s to lower\n60s.",
